  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n- Areas of Frost Possible Tonight into Tuesday Morning\n\nSome patchy frost is possible this morning as temperatures may dip\naround 37 along and north of US-10. Otherwise better chances exist\ntonight for more scattered to widespread frost. Depending on the\nensemble, probabilities vary. The highest chances (30 to 50 \npercent) for frost are in inland areas north of M-20. Dew points\nin this area get into the low 30s. With the inversion in place\ntonight along with 925 mb temperatures around 6 C and light winds,\nlow temperatures could drop into the mid 30s. Lower chances exist\nfurther south and along the lakeshore. With some uncertainty in\nplace, held off on issuing any frost headlines at this time.\n\n- Mostly Dry Conditions Into Weekend\n\nWith limited moisture this week, dry conditions are expected to\npersist into the weekend. However with a couple shortwave troughs\nwithin the northwest flow aloft, we'll need to monitor for some\nslight rain chances. The first period is Tuesday morning as a\ndecaying area of showers moves in from Wisconsin. Dry air should\nerode these showers before they make it to the lakeshore. The next\nis Tuesday night into Wednesday when warm air advection and the\nlow level jet move in ahead of the front. Overall the better\nmoisture and lift will be north of the area, but there is a signal\nfor increased mid level moisture with the current model runs.\nWe'll see if this trend continues, if it does PoPs would need to\nbe added to the forecast.\n\nHigh temperatures this week will be in the 60s to low 70s with\novernight temperatures in the 30s and 40s. Tuesday night will be\nthe warmest with lows in the 50s.",
